+# Reproduction changes
+
+Source baselines: SliceIt `3a69648`, DiSECt `b8d0e58`, robot utilities
+`7cd7cb8`, tf2rl `a8d41f6`.
+
+| Interface | Source behavior | Current implementation |
+|---|---|---|
+| Episode reset | Reads observation before moving the robot and resetting DiSECt | Reads observation after physical reset; finite-difference history starts at the new pose |
+| Force-history reward | Observation is component-major; slicing reward reshapes it as time-major | Transpose the decoded history before applying the existing component scales and reward formula |
+| Force-history actor | Component-major observations are reshaped directly into TCN time-major input | Decode components then transpose to time-major; network layers and widths unchanged |
+| Replay priority | Column reward/done broadcasts against vector Q into a batch-by-batch TD array | One reward, done and TD error per transition, matching the critic update shape |
+| Material-force wiring | README launch omits the filter's secondary input and places the controller remap after Gazebo inclusion | Existing `31852dc` relay connects base-expressed force to the wrist sensor filter; remap is applied before Gazebo starts |
+
+Material force retains the author's sum of contact-force magnitudes and
+single-axis zero-torque construction in `ROSVisualizer.step_simulation()`.
+No contact moment or new application point is added. Materials, task thresholds,
+reward coefficients, SAC learning rates, warmup and batch size retain the public
+scene configuration described in [PUBLIC_DATA](../PUBLIC_DATA.md).
+
+The source trainer updates during random-action warmup. The critic currently
+ignores replay importance weights, and the force-history reader takes the oldest
+requested entries of its 25-sample queue. These source behaviors are unchanged.
+
+The shared SuperMimic interface uses the corrected force-history decoding,
+paused reset joint snapshots, and read-only native controller/filter state,
+including the FDCC internal velocity. Its current sampling contract is
+`synchronous_paused_joint_snapshot_v1`.
+
+## Stepped transition ordering
+
+| Interface | Source behavior | Current implementation |
+|---|---|---|
+| Step-control flag | The direct DiSECt environment inherited a false flag despite the existing configuration | Base environment reads the existing flag, matching the arm |
+| Reset pause | Pause/unpause publish asynchronous world-control requests; queued joint messages can change the first observation cache | Synchronous physics services and explicit snapshot_only pause/read before the reset observation; no physical tick is added |
+| Controller parameters | Gain RPC threads are still pending at the first physics step | Stepped mode finishes parameter writes before motion; continuous mode retains asynchronous updates |
+| Gazebo step completion | StepControl returns after publishing a request | Native `World::Step(n)` completes before return and reports start/end simulation times |
+| Knife joint sample | FK uses asynchronous joint topics sampled before integration | StepControl returns ordered post-integration joint position/velocity/effort at end_time; client installs atomically and rejects late/equal-time topic updates |
+| Control duration | ROS-clock/float loop sometimes executes 52 ms for a 50 ms action | Accumulate authoritative step durations as integer nanoseconds; waypoint and force conditions unchanged |
+| Native memory | Solver/filter recurrence is omitted from recorded state | Read-only controller/filter snapshots and atomic full sensor queue reads |
+
+The existing 2 ms robot/material coupling, gains, action mapping, material and
+reward parameters remain unchanged. Rebuild `gazebo_msgs` and `gazebo_ros` in
+underlay_ws together after the StepControl response change. Native state checks:
+`scripts/check_native_fdcc_state.py --output runs/native_fdcc_check` after
+sourcing `scripts/public_env.sh`.
+Source: https://github.com/gazebosim/gazebo/blob/gazebo11_11.15.1/gazebo/physics/World.cc#L807
+
+The two-tick paused loop cannot rely on a fresh JointState topic: the source
+joint-state controller may skip publication when its realtime publisher is busy.
+A service response now carries the selected hinge/slider joint snapshot directly.
+Angular coordinates retain the ROS hardware continuous branch. Episode reset
+uses the existing NO_RESET_SIM lifecycle, so simulation timestamps remain
+monotonic; recreating a world also requires recreating its state consumer.
+The snapshot follows the completed integration, while the original ROS hardware
+read is on WorldUpdateBegin. This changes the sample phase by up to one physics
+tick, without changing the requested tick count or physical equations.
+Source: https://github.com/ros-controls/ros_controllers/blob/noetic-devel/joint_state_controller/src/joint_state_controller.cpp
+
+`StepControl.snapshot_only=true` requires zero steps and nonempty joint names.
+It pauses and returns an authoritative joint sample with identical start/end
+times. The UR robot reset installs this sample before its first observation;
+queued topic updates at or before that time are ignored. Default zero-step
+requests retain their unpause behavior.
+
+
+### Training shutdown
+The active Gazebo connection provides a strict shutdown unpause service call
+with a 5 s wall-time bound. Controller shutdown has the same bound and reports
+failed service acknowledgements. Training orchestration independently attempts
+checkpoint/replay save, environment close, local status and W&B finish, retaining
+the original training exception. These bounds apply to teardown; robot/material
+integration, reward and learner parameters are unchanged.
